# Builds and compares the benchmark binaries of two revisions of this repository.
#
#   bin/check-perf metadata [BUILD]      describe the machine, and the compiler of BUILD (default
#                                        build), as JSON
#   bin/check-perf build REV DIR         build the benchmarks of REV in DIR, in Release, from a
#                                        detached worktree beside DIR that is removed afterwards
#   bin/check-perf compare BASE HEAD     run BASE and HEAD in alternating rounds and give each key a
#                                        verdict; each side is a build directory or a revision
#   bin/check-perf ci                    compare in a GitHub Actions run, as described below
#   bin/check-perf comment DIR...        render the result.json of each DIR as the one comment of the
#                                        pull request, as described below
#   bin/check-perf publish COMMIT DIR... add the head rows of the result.json of each DIR as those of
#                                        COMMIT to refs/site/pages, and render perf/index.html there
#   bin/check-perf site FROM TO          copy the perf data and the ABI reports of FROM, a checkout of
#                                        refs/site/pages, to TO, and render perf/index.html,
#                                        abi/index.html and the root index.html there
#   bin/check-perf --self-test           check the verdict rules, the reports, ci, comment, publish,
#                                        site and the metadata shape
#
# compare writes result.json, report.txt, comment.md and head.bmf.json to --out. A round runs every
# selected binary of both sides. Base runs first in even rounds and head first in odd ones, so a
# drift over the run hits both sides alike. Each key gets the per-round ratios head/base of its
# median latency, and a verdict:
#
#   faster          the upper bound of the 95% CI of the median ratio is at most 1 - MIN_EFFECT
#   slower          the lower bound of that CI is at least 1 + MIN_EFFECT
#   likely faster   the upper bound of that CI is below 1 but above 1 - MIN_EFFECT
#   likely slower   the lower bound of that CI is above 1 but below 1 + MIN_EFFECT
#   noisy           (max - min) / median of the base rounds' medians exceeds NOISY, whatever the
#                   ratios say; or fewer than MIN_PAIRED rounds measured the key on both sides,
#                   too few ratios for a 95% CI
#   no change       otherwise: the CI contains 1
#   new, removed    the key is measured only at head, or only at base; a new key that failed at
#                   base is noted "base failed"
#   failed          a head round reported the case failed
#
# Each key also gets a CPU verdict by the same rules, from the per-round ratios head/base of its
# process CPU time per operation, noisy on the base rounds' spread of it. It has none when either
# side lacks the CPU time, as on a platform that cannot provide it, or when the key failed at head.
#
# A per-operation key also gets a throughput verdict, from its operations per second over all
# threads. On several threads a change can raise the latency of each operation and still raise the
# total throughput, by contending less. The verdict applies the same rules to the per-round ratios
# base/head of throughput, so more throughput at head reads faster; noisy is on the base rounds'
# spread of throughput. The change shown is head/base, so +% is more throughput. A batched key has
# no throughput, and a key without it at either side, or failed at head, has no throughput verdict.
#
# A row is noted "suspect" when a round at either side reported the key suspect, a batched median
# under 0.5 ns per iteration, which indicates that the compiler removed the work. The key has no
# BMF entry.
#
# A row is marked harness_changed when base and head are revisions and the framework sources, or the
# case file of its binary and the headers that file includes from test/benchmark/, differ.
#
# A result keyed by a bare case name is keyed SUITE/CASE instead. Such a result is a skip or a
# failure recorded before its case reached a key. SUITE is the binary's name without benchmark_.
# Binaries share case names, such as kv_get in kv_fake and kv_cluster, and this keeps their
# results apart. Both sides are keyed alike, so their rows pair. A key reported twice in a round,
# by one binary or by two, is an error.
#
# ci compares HEAD with a base picked from the event in GITHUB_EVENT_NAME, GITHUB_EVENT_PATH and
# EVENT_BEFORE: HEAD^1 for a pull request, whose HEAD is the merge commit, and for a manual run;
# EVENT_BEFORE, the previous head of the branch, for a push. A base that cannot be resolved, such as
# the all-zero id of a new branch, leaves every case new. On a pull request ci also writes the pull
# request number to pr-number in --out.
#
# comment reads the artifacts of a perf run of a pull request, which is untrusted input. It reads
# only pr-number and result.json of each DIR. pr-number must be a positive integer, and the pull
# request must still be at the HEAD_SHA of the run. The comment is rendered from result.json by the
# code of this checkout, after these checks:
#
#   key        '/'-separated [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* segments, as the benchmark runner requires
#   sha        7 to 40 hex digits, else rendered as ?
#   verdict    one of VERDICTS; a base status one of BASE_STATUSES
#   number     a finite Numeric
#   free text  letters, digits, space and ._-+()/,@ only, every @ and www. neutralised, at most
#              TEXT_LIMIT characters
#
# A row or field that fails its check is left out. A result.json that is not an object with rows,
# base and head fails the command. The body is the marker line, then the comment of each DIR in
# order, cut to 65,000 characters. It replaces the comment that github-actions[bot] wrote with that
# marker, else creates one. It needs GH_TOKEN and GITHUB_REPOSITORY, and does nothing when no DIR
# has a result.json.
#
# site renders the pages from data only: perf/data/*.json and abi/reports/COMMIT/report-*.txt are
# copied, and every other file of FROM, an index.html included, is left behind. abi/index.html is
# rendered by bin/check-abi report, which needs GH_TOKEN and GITHUB_REPOSITORY.
#
# Exit status: 0 done, 1 a case failed at head, 3 usage or environment error, 4 the revision has no
# test/benchmark/, 5 the build failed.

require "English"
require "etc"
require "fileutils"
require "json"
require "net/http"
require "open3"
require "optparse"
require "stringio"
require "time"
require "tmpdir"
require "uri"

require_relative "pages_site"

module CheckPerf
  class Error < StandardError
    def status = 3
  end

  class MissingError < Error
    def status = 4
  end

  class BuildError < Error
    def status = 5
  end

  # With 7 rounds the CI of the median ratio spans the smallest to the largest ratio. An unchanged
  # key then has every ratio on one side of 1.0 with probability 2/128. That bounds the rate of a
  # likely verdict per unchanged key.
  ROUNDS = 7
  # The fewest per-round ratios with a 95% CI of their median; fewer give no verdict but noisy.
  MIN_PAIRED = 6
  # The change that the CI bound nearest 1.0 must reach for faster or slower.
  MIN_EFFECT = 0.02
  # The largest spread of the base rounds, relative to their median, that still gives a verdict.
  NOISY = 0.10
  # comment.md stays under GitHub's 65,536-character comment limit with room for a note.
  COMMENT_LIMIT = 60_000

  CMAKE_OPTIONS = %w[
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    -DCOUCHBASE_CXX_CLIENT_BUILD_TESTS=ON
    -DCOUCHBASE_CXX_CLIENT_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF
    -DCOUCHBASE_CXX_CLIENT_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF
    -DCOUCHBASE_CXX_CLIENT_BUILD_DOCS=OFF
    -DCOUCHBASE_CXX_CLIENT_BUILD_FIT_PERFORMER=OFF
    -DCOUCHBASE_CXX_CLIENT_BUILD_COUCHBASE2=OFF
  ].freeze

  # The sources every benchmark binary shares. A change to any of them changes every row.
  FRAMEWORK = %w[
    test/benchmark/bench_*
    test/benchmark/histogram.hxx
    test/benchmark/statistics.*
    test/benchmark/report.*
    test/benchmark/resource_usage.*
    test/framework/
    cmake/TestFramework.cmake
  ].freeze

  def self.run(*args, chdir: nil, quiet: false)
    warn args.join(" ") unless quiet
    out, err, status = Open3.capture3(*args, **(chdir ? {chdir: chdir} : {}))
    raise Error, "#{args.join(' ')} failed with #{status}:\n#{err}" unless status.success?

    out
  end

  def self.read(path)
    File.read(path).strip
  rescue SystemCallError
    nil
  end

  # The zero-based ranks k and n-1-k of the distribution-free 95% CI of a median. k is the largest
  # rank with 2 * P(Bin(n, 1/2) <= k) <= 0.05, as median_with_ci in test/benchmark/statistics.cxx
  # computes it. nil when no rank qualifies, which is every n < 6.
  def self.ci_ranks(n)
    log_term = -n * Math.log(2)
    tail = 0.0
    rank = nil
    (0...n).each do |k|
      tail += Math.exp(log_term)
      break if 2 * tail > 0.05

      rank = k
      log_term += Math.log((n - k).to_f / (k + 1))
    end
    rank && [rank, n - 1 - rank]
  end

  # lower and upper are nil when the values are too few for a 95% CI.
  def self.median_ci(values)
    sorted = values.sort
    n = sorted.size
    lower, upper = ci_ranks(n)
    {median: (sorted[(n - 1) / 2] + sorted[n / 2]) / 2.0, lower: lower && sorted[lower], upper: upper && sorted[upper]}
  end

  def self.spread(values)
    (values.max - values.min) / median_ci(values)[:median]
  end

  # ratios are head/base per round, base_medians the base rounds' medians.
  def self.verdict(ratios, base_medians)
    return "noisy" if spread(base_medians) > NOISY || ratios.size < MIN_PAIRED

    ci = median_ci(ratios)
    return "faster" if ci[:upper] <= 1 - MIN_EFFECT
    return "slower" if ci[:lower] >= 1 + MIN_EFFECT
    return "likely faster" if ci[:upper] < 1
    return "likely slower" if ci[:lower] > 1

    "no change"
  end

  # The sides in the order round runs them.
  def self.order(round) = round.even? ? %i[base head] : %i[head base]

  MEASURED = %w[measured suspect].freeze

  # base and head hold one case of the result JSON, or nil, per round. Returns nil for a key that
  # neither side measured, such as one skipped on both.
  def self.row(key, base, head, harness_changed: false)
    measured = ->(c) { c && MEASURED.include?(c["status"]) && c.dig("latency_ns", "median").to_f.positive? }
    summary = lambda do |cases|
      medians = cases.select(&measured).map { |c| c.dig("latency_ns", "median").to_f }
      medians.empty? ? nil : median_ci(medians)
    end
    errors = ->(cases) { cases.compact.sum { |c| c["errors"].to_i } }
    pairs = base.zip(head).select { |b, h| measured.call(b) && measured.call(h) }
    ratios = pairs.map { |b, h| h.dig("latency_ns", "median").to_f / b.dig("latency_ns", "median") }
    reason = nil
    base_summary = summary.call(base)
    head_summary = summary.call(head)
    verdict = if head.any? { |c| c && c["status"] == "failed" }
                "failed"
              elsif !base_summary && !head_summary
                return nil
              elsif !base_summary
                reason = "base failed" if base.any? { |c| c && c["status"] == "failed" }
                "new"
              elsif !head_summary
                "removed"
              elsif ratios.empty?
                reason = "no round measured it at both base and head"
                "noisy"
              elsif ratios.size < MIN_PAIRED
                reason = "fewer than #{MIN_PAIRED} paired rounds"
                "noisy"
              else
                verdict(ratios, base.select(&measured).map { |c| c.dig("latency_ns", "median").to_f })
              end
    ci_of = ->(values) { values.empty? ? nil : median_ci(values) }
    cpu = ->(c) { (v = measured.call(c) && c["cpu_ns_per_op"]).is_a?(Numeric) && v.positive? ? v.to_f : nil }
    cpu_ratios = base.zip(head).filter_map { |b, h| (x = cpu.call(b)) && (y = cpu.call(h)) && y / x }
    base_cpu = base.filter_map(&cpu)
    rss = lambda do |cases|
      ci_of.call(cases.filter_map { |c| (v = measured.call(c) && c["max_rss_bytes"]).is_a?(Integer) ? v : nil })&.dig(:median)&.round
    end
    ops = ->(c) { (v = measured.call(c) && c["ops_per_second"]).is_a?(Numeric) && v.positive? ? v.to_f : nil }
    ops_pairs = base.zip(head).filter_map { |b, h| (x = ops.call(b)) && (y = ops.call(h)) && [x, y] }
    base_ops = base.filter_map(&ops)
    head_ops = head.filter_map(&ops)
    result = {key: key, verdict: verdict, base: base_summary, head: head_summary,
              ratio: ci_of.call(ratios),
              cpu: {verdict: cpu_ratios.empty? || verdict == "failed" ? nil : verdict(cpu_ratios, base_cpu),
                    base: ci_of.call(base_cpu), head: ci_of.call(head.filter_map(&cpu)), ratio: ci_of.call(cpu_ratios)},
              max_rss_bytes: {base: rss.call(base), head: rss.call(head)},
              harness_changed: harness_changed,
              errors: {base: errors.call(base), head: errors.call(head)},
              suspect: (base + head).any? { |c| c && c["status"] == "suspect" }}
    unless base_ops.empty? && head_ops.empty?
      result[:throughput] = {verdict: ops_pairs.empty? || verdict == "failed" ? nil : verdict(ops_pairs.map { |x, y| x / y }, base_ops),
                             base: ci_of.call(base_ops), head: ci_of.call(head_ops),
                             ratio: ci_of.call(ops_pairs.map { |x, y| y / x })}
    end
    result[:reason] = reason if reason
    result
  end

  # listings maps a binary to the keys of its result JSON.
  def self.check_unique(listings)
    owner = {}
    listings.each do |binary, keys|
      keys.each do |key|
        raise Error, "#{key} is reported twice by #{binary}" if owner[key] == binary
        raise Error, "#{key} is registered by both #{owner[key]} and #{binary}" if owner[key]

        owner[key] = binary
      end
    end
  end

  def self.suite_name(binary) = File.basename(binary, ".exe").delete_prefix("benchmark_")

  def self.metadata(build = "build")
    cpuinfo = read("/proc/cpuinfo").to_s
    cpus = Dir["/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu[0-9]*/topology"]
    cores = cpus.map { |t| read("#{t}/core_cpus_list") || read("#{t}/thread_siblings_list") }.compact.uniq
    logical = cpuinfo.scan(/^processor\s*:/).size
    logical = Etc.nprocessors if logical.zero?
    smt = read("/sys/devices/system/cpu/smt/active")
    meminfo = read("/proc/meminfo").to_s
    kib = ->(field) { (v = meminfo[/^#{field}:\s+(\d+) kB/, 1]) && v.to_i * 1024 }
    fstype, device = (Open3.capture2("findmnt", "-n", "-o", "FSTYPE,SOURCE", "-T", ".").first.split rescue [])
    cache = read(File.join(build.to_s, "CMakeCache.txt")).to_s
    compiler = cache[/^CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER:\w+=(.+)$/, 1]
    version = compiler && (Open3.capture2(compiler, "--version").first.lines.first&.strip rescue nil)
    {
      cpu: {model: cpuinfo[/^model name\s*:\s*(.+)$/, 1], logical_cores: logical,
            physical_cores: cores.empty? ? nil : cores.size,
            governor: read("/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_governor"),
            smt: smt ? smt == "1" : !cores.empty? && logical > cores.size},
      memory: {total_bytes: kib.call("MemTotal"), available_bytes: kib.call("MemAvailable")},
      disk: {filesystem: fstype, device: device},
      network: Dir["/sys/class/net/*/mtu"].sort.to_h { |f| [File.basename(File.dirname(f)), read(f).to_i] },
      os: {kernel: Etc.uname[:release], distribution: read("/etc/os-release").to_s[/^PRETTY_NAME="?([^"\n]*)/, 1]},
      runner: %w[ImageOS ImageVersion RUNNER_NAME].to_h { |k| [k, ENV.fetch(k, nil)] },
      compiler: compiler && {path: compiler, version: version},
      build_type: cache[/^CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE:\w+=(.*)$/, 1],
    }
  end

  def self.sha(rev) = run("git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "#{rev}^{commit}", quiet: true).strip

  # Builds the benchmarks of rev in dir and returns its commit id. A dir already built from that
  # commit is reused. The worktree is checked out at check-perf-src next to dir and removed before
  # returning. Builds sharing a parent directory must not run concurrently, since they share
  # check-perf-src. The parent is also CCACHE_BASEDIR: every build compiles the same relative
  # source paths, so ccache can reuse one build's objects in another.
  def self.build(rev, dir)
    sha = sha(rev)
    dir = File.expand_path(dir)
    stamp = File.join(dir, "check-perf.sha")
    return sha if read(stamp) == sha
    unless system("git", "cat-file", "-e", "#{sha}:test/benchmark", err: File::NULL)
      raise MissingError, "#{sha[0, 12]} has no test/benchmark/"
    end

    source = File.join(File.dirname(dir), "check-perf-src")
    system("git", "worktree", "remove", "--force", source, err: File::NULL) if File.directory?(source)
    raise Error, "#{source} is in the way of the worktree" if File.exist?(source)

    begin
      run("git", "worktree", "add", "--detach", source, sha)
      # cmake/Cache.cmake sets the compiler launcher itself, preferring sccache, so ccache is chosen
      # through its CACHE_OPTION.
      launcher = system("ccache", "--version", out: File::NULL) ? %w[-DCACHE_OPTION=ccache] : []
      generator = system("ninja", "--version", out: File::NULL) ? %w[-G Ninja] : []
      env = {"CCACHE_BASEDIR" => File.dirname(dir)}
      [["cmake", "-S", source, "-B", dir, *generator, *CMAKE_OPTIONS, *launcher],
       ["cmake", "--build", dir, "--config", "Release", "--target", "build_benchmarks"]].each do |args|
        warn args.join(" ")
        raise BuildError, "#{sha[0, 12]} failed to build: #{args.join(' ')}" unless system(env, *args, out: $stderr)
      end
      File.write(stamp, sha)
    ensure
      system("git", "worktree", "remove", "--force", source, err: File::NULL) if File.directory?(source)
      FileUtils.rm_rf(source)
      system("git", "worktree", "prune")
    end
    sha
  end

  def self.binaries(dir)
    list = File.join(dir, "benchmarks.txt")
    unless File.file?(list)
      lists = Dir[File.join(dir, "benchmarks_*.txt")]
      list = lists.find { |f| f.end_with?("_Release.txt") } || (lists.size == 1 ? lists.first : nil)
      raise Error, "#{dir} has no benchmarks.txt" unless list
    end
    File.readlines(list, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?)
  end

  def self.build_dir?(arg)
    File.file?(File.join(arg, "benchmarks.txt")) || !Dir[File.join(arg, "benchmarks_*.txt")].empty?
  end

  # A side of compare: its label, commit id when it is a revision, status, and binaries by suite.
  Side = Struct.new(:label, :sha, :status, :reason, :binaries)

  def self.side(arg, work, suites, required:)
    if build_dir?(arg)
      dir = File.expand_path(arg)
      sha = nil
      label = dir
    else
      begin
        sha = sha(arg)
      rescue Error => e
        raise if required

        return Side.new(arg, nil, "missing", "#{arg} cannot be resolved: #{(e.message.lines.last || e.class.name).strip}", {})
      end
      label = sha
      begin
        dir = File.join(work, "build-#{sha[0, 12]}")
        build(sha, dir)
      rescue MissingError, BuildError => e
        raise if required

        return Side.new(label, sha, e.is_a?(MissingError) ? "missing" : "failed", e.message, {})
      end
    end
    found = binaries(dir).to_h { |b| [suite_name(b), b] }
    unknown = suites - found.keys
    raise Error, "#{label} has no benchmark_#{unknown.join(', benchmark_')}" if required && !unknown.empty?

    found.select! { |name, _| suites.include?(name) } unless suites.empty?
    Side.new(label, sha, "built", nil, found)
  end

  # Runs one binary and returns the result JSON it writes to json. Exit 1 and 77 are results, not
  # errors. A binary that stops before writing its results also exits 1, so a missing result file is
  # the error.
  def self.measure(binary, json, bmf: nil)
    FileUtils.rm_f(json)
    args = [binary, "--json", json, *(bmf ? ["--bmf", bmf] : [])]
    _, err, status = Open3.capture3(*args)
    unless [0, 1, 77].include?(status.exitstatus) && File.file?(json)
      missing = File.file?(json) ? "" : " and wrote no results"
      raise Error, "#{args.join(' ')} exited with #{status.exitstatus}#{missing}:\n#{err}"
    end

    JSON.parse(File.read(json))
  end

  # The test/benchmark/ files a suite's binary is built from, besides the framework: its case file,
  # the headers it includes from test/benchmark/, and their .cxx files.
  def self.case_files(sha, suite)
    main = "test/benchmark/#{suite}.cxx"
    text, status = Open3.capture2("git", "show", "#{sha}:#{main}", err: File::NULL)
    return [main] unless status.success?

    headers = text.scan(/^#include "([^"\/]+)\.hxx"/).flatten
    [main, *headers.flat_map { |h| ["test/benchmark/#{h}.hxx", "test/benchmark/#{h}.cxx"] }]
  end

  def self.differs?(base, head, paths)
    _, status = Open3.capture2("git", "diff", "--quiet", base, head, "--", *paths)
    return false if status.exitstatus.zero?
    return true if status.exitstatus == 1

    raise Error, "git diff #{base} #{head} failed with #{status}"
  end

  def self.compare(base_arg, head_arg, rounds:, suites:, out:, work: ".perf-work")
    head = side(head_arg, work, suites, required: true)
    base = side(base_arg, work, suites, required: false)
    raise Error, "#{head.label} has no benchmark to run" if head.binaries.empty?

    results = {base: Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = Array.new(rounds) }, head: Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = Array.new(rounds) }}
    suite_of = {}
    server = nil
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(out)
    bmf = {}
    Dir.mktmpdir("check-perf") do |tmp|
      rounds.times do |round|
        order(round).each do |name|
          s = name == :base ? base : head
          keys = {}
          s.binaries.each do |suite, binary|
            warn "round #{round + 1}/#{rounds}: #{name} #{suite}"
            bmf_file = name == :head && round == rounds - 1 ? File.join(tmp, "#{suite}.bmf.json") : nil
            result = measure(binary, File.join(tmp, "#{name}-#{round}-#{suite}.json"), bmf: bmf_file)
            bmf.merge!(JSON.parse(File.read(bmf_file))) if bmf_file
            server ||= result["server"] if name == :head
            cases = result["cases"].map { |c| c["key"].to_s.include?("/") ? c : c.merge("key" => "#{suite}/#{c['key']}") }
            keys[binary] = cases.map { |c| c["key"] }
            cases.each do |c|
              results[name][c["key"]][round] = c
              suite_of[c["key"]] ||= suite
            end
          end
          check_unique(keys) if round.zero?
        end
      end
    end

    whole = base.sha && head.sha && differs?(base.sha, head.sha, FRAMEWORK)
    changed = Hash.new do |h, suite|
      h[suite] = whole || (base.sha && head.sha &&
                           differs?(base.sha, head.sha, (case_files(base.sha, suite) + case_files(head.sha, suite)).uniq)) || false
    end
    keys = (results[:base].keys + results[:head].keys).uniq.sort
    rows = keys.filter_map do |key|
      row(key, results[:base].fetch(key, Array.new(rounds)), results[:head].fetch(key, Array.new(rounds)),
          harness_changed: changed[suite_of[key]])
    end
    skipped = keys - rows.map { |r| r[:key] }

    meta = metadata(head.sha ? File.join(work, "build-#{head.sha[0, 12]}") : head_arg)
    meta[:cluster] = {nodes: server["nodes"], version: server["version"]} if server
    result = {schema: 1, base: {sha: base.sha || base.label, status: base.status},
              head: {sha: head.sha || head.label}, suites: head.binaries.keys.sort, rounds: rounds, metadata: meta,
              rows: rows, skipped: skipped}
    result[:base][:reason] = base.reason if base.reason
    File.write(File.join(out, "result.json"), "#{JSON.pretty_generate(result)}\n")
    File.write(File.join(out, "head.bmf.json"), "#{JSON.pretty_generate(bmf)}\n")
    File.write(File.join(out, "report.txt"), report(result))
    File.write(File.join(out, "comment.md"), comment(result))
    print report(result)
    rows.any? { |r| r[:verdict] == "failed" } ? 1 : 0
  end

  def self.duration(ns)
    return "-" unless ns

    unit, scale = [["s", 1_000_000_000.0], ["ms", 1_000_000.0], ["µs", 1_000.0]].find { |_, s| ns >= s } || ["ns", 1]
    v = ns / scale
    format(v >= 100 ? "%.0f %s" : v >= 10 ? "%.1f %s" : "%.2f %s", v, unit)
  end

  # Operations per second with k and M, as the benchmark runner prints them.
  def self.rate(v)
    return "-" unless v

    value, suffix = v >= 1_000_000 ? [v / 1_000_000.0, "M"] : v >= 1_000 ? [v / 1_000.0, "k"] : [v, ""]
    format(value >= 100 ? "%.0f%s" : value >= 10 ? "%.1f%s" : "%.2f%s", value, suffix)
  end

  def self.change(ratio)
    return "-" unless ratio

    pct = ->(r) { format("%+.1f%%", (r - 1) * 100) }
    return pct[ratio[:median]] unless ratio[:lower]

    "#{pct[ratio[:median]]} [#{pct[ratio[:lower]]}, #{pct[ratio[:upper]]}]"
  end

  def self.verdict_text(row)
    notes = [row[:reason], row[:harness_changed] ? "harness changed" : nil, row[:suspect] ? "suspect" : nil].compact
    notes.empty? ? row[:verdict] : "#{row[:verdict]} (#{notes.join('; ')})"
  end

  def self.short(sha) = sha.to_s.match?(/\A\h{40}\z/) ? sha[0, 12] : sha

  def self.machine(meta)
    cpu = meta[:cpu]
    gib = meta.dig(:memory, :total_bytes).to_f / 2**30
    runner = meta[:runner].values_at("ImageOS", "ImageVersion").compact.join(" ")
    ["CPU: #{cpu[:model]}, #{cpu[:logical_cores]} logical / #{cpu[:physical_cores]} physical cores, " \
     "SMT #{cpu[:smt] ? 'on' : 'off'}, governor #{cpu[:governor] || 'unknown'}",
     "Memory: #{format('%.1f', gib)} GiB; runner: #{runner.empty? ? 'local' : runner}; " \
     "compiler: #{meta.dig(:compiler, :version) || 'unknown'}; build type: #{meta[:build_type] || 'unknown'}",
     *(meta[:cluster] ? ["Cluster: #{meta[:cluster][:nodes]} nodes, server #{meta[:cluster][:version]}"] : [])]
  end

  # The reason of a base that was not built names the revision and why: no test/benchmark/, a
  # revision that cannot be resolved, or a failed build.
  def self.base_note(result)
    base = result[:base]
    "Every case is new: #{base[:reason]}." unless base[:status] == "built"
  end

  # The bound on the rate of a likely verdict per unchanged key over n rounds, 2 * P(Bin(n, 1/2) <= k)
  # with k = ci_ranks(n), as [numerator, 2**n]. nil when n rounds give no CI.
  def self.likely_rate(n)
    k, = n.is_a?(Integer) ? ci_ranks(n) : nil
    return nil unless k

    [2 * (0..k).sum { |i| (0...i).reduce(1) { |c, j| c * (n - j) / (j + 1) } }, 2**n]
  end

  def self.likely_note(result)
    return unless result[:rows].any? { |r| [r[:verdict], r.dig(:cpu, :verdict), r.dig(:throughput, :verdict)].compact.any? { |v| v.start_with?("likely") } }

    rate = likely_rate(result[:rounds]) or return
    "A likely row is a hint to re-run: with #{result[:rounds]} rounds an unchanged key reads likely with " \
      "probability at most #{rate.join('/')}."
  end

  CPU_HEADER = ["base cpu/op", "head cpu/op", "cpu change [95% CI]", "cpu verdict"].freeze

  def self.cpu_columns(row)
    cpu = row[:cpu] || {}
    [duration(cpu.dig(:base, :median)), duration(cpu.dig(:head, :median)), change(cpu[:ratio]), cpu[:verdict] || "-"]
  end

  THROUGHPUT_HEADER = ["base ops/s", "head ops/s", "ops/s change [95% CI]", "ops/s verdict"].freeze

  def self.throughput_columns(row)
    ops = row[:throughput] || {}
    [rate(ops.dig(:base, :median)), rate(ops.dig(:head, :median)), change(ops[:ratio]), ops[:verdict] || "-"]
  end

  def self.report(result)
    rows = result[:rows].map do |r|
      [r[:key], duration(r.dig(:base, :median)), duration(r.dig(:head, :median)), change(r[:ratio]),
       verdict_text(r), *cpu_columns(r), *throughput_columns(r),
       r[:errors].values.sum.positive? ? "errors #{r[:errors][:base]} / #{r[:errors][:head]}" : ""]
    end
    header = ["key", "base p50", "head p50", "change [95% CI]", "verdict", *CPU_HEADER, *THROUGHPUT_HEADER, ""]
    widths = header.each_index.map { |i| ([header] + rows).map { |row| row[i].length }.max }
    table = ([header] + rows).map { |row| row.each_with_index.map { |c, i| c.ljust(widths[i]) }.join("  ").rstrip }
    [
      "base #{short(result[:base][:sha])} (#{result[:base][:status]}), head #{short(result[:head][:sha])}, " \
      "rounds: #{result[:rounds]}",
      *machine(result[:metadata]),
      *base_note(result),
      "",
      *table,
      *(result[:skipped].empty? ? [] : ["", "Skipped at base and head: #{result[:skipped].join(', ')}"]),
      *(likely_note(result) ? ["", likely_note(result)] : []),
      "",
    ].join("\n")
  end

  def self.comment(result)
    head = [
      "### Benchmarks of #{result[:suites].join(', ')}: base `#{short(result[:base][:sha])}`, " \
      "head `#{short(result[:head][:sha])}`",
      "",
      *machine(result[:metadata]).map { |l| "#{l}  " },
      *(base_note(result) ? ["", base_note(result)] : []),
      "",
      "| key | base p50 | head p50 | change [95% CI] | verdict | #{(CPU_HEADER + THROUGHPUT_HEADER).join(' | ')} |",
      "|#{'---|' * (5 + CPU_HEADER.size + THROUGHPUT_HEADER.size)}",
    ]
    text = "#{head.join("\n")}\n"
    result[:rows].each_with_index do |r, i|
      line = "| `#{r[:key]}` | #{duration(r.dig(:base, :median))} | #{duration(r.dig(:head, :median))} | " \
             "#{change(r[:ratio])} | #{verdict_text(r)} | #{(cpu_columns(r) + throughput_columns(r)).join(' | ')} |\n"
      if text.length + line.length > COMMENT_LIMIT
        text += "\n#{result[:rows].size - i} more rows are in result.json.\n"
        break
      end
      text += line
    end
    text += "\nSkipped at base and head: #{result[:skipped].map { |k| "`#{k}`" }.join(', ')}\n" unless result[:skipped].empty?
    text += "\n#{likely_note(result)}\n" if likely_note(result)
    text
  end

  NULL_SHA = "0" * 40

  # The [base, head, pull request number] that ci compares for an event.
  def self.ci_plan(event, payload, before)
    case event
    when "pull_request"
      number = payload.is_a?(Hash) ? payload.dig("pull_request", "number") : nil
      raise Error, "the pull_request event has no pull_request.number" unless number.is_a?(Integer)

      ["HEAD^1", "HEAD", number]
    when "push" then [before.to_s.empty? ? NULL_SHA : before, "HEAD", nil]
    when "workflow_dispatch" then ["HEAD^1", "HEAD", nil]
    else raise Error, "ci does not handle the event #{event.inspect}"
    end
  end

  def self.ci_event(env)
    name = env["GITHUB_EVENT_NAME"].to_s
    path = env["GITHUB_EVENT_PATH"].to_s
    raise Error, "ci needs GITHUB_EVENT_NAME and GITHUB_EVENT_PATH, which GitHub Actions sets" if name.empty? || path.empty?

    [name, JSON.parse(File.read(path))]
  rescue SystemCallError, JSON::ParserError => e
    raise Error, "cannot read the event payload #{path}: #{e.message}"
  end

  def self.ci(**options)
    name, payload = ci_event(ENV)
    base, head, number = ci_plan(name, payload, ENV.fetch("EVENT_BEFORE", nil))
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(options.fetch(:out))
    File.write(File.join(options.fetch(:out), "pr-number"), "#{number}\n") if number
    compare(base, head, **options)
  end

  MARKER = "<!-- check-perf -->"
  # GitHub rejects a comment body longer than 65,536 characters.
  BODY_LIMIT = 65_000
  TRUNCATED = "\n\nThe comment was truncated; the full reports are in the artifacts of the perf run.\n"
  BOT_LOGIN = "github-actions[bot]"

  # Calls the GitHub REST API as the token's owner: call(verb, path, body = nil) returns the parsed
  # response. The self-test passes a lambda of the same shape instead.
  class GitHubHttp
    def initialize(token) = @token = token

    def call(verb, path, body = nil)
      uri = URI("https://api.github.com#{path}")
      req = Net::HTTP.const_get(verb.to_s.capitalize).new(uri)
      req["Accept"] = "application/vnd.github+json"
      req["Authorization"] = "Bearer #{@token}"
      req.body = JSON.generate(body) if body
      res = Net::HTTP.start(uri.host, uri.port, use_ssl: true) { |http| http.request(req) }
      raise Error, "GitHub #{verb.to_s.upcase} #{path}: #{res.code}" unless res.is_a?(Net::HTTPSuccess)

      JSON.parse(res.body)
    end
  end

  # An artifact comes from a run of pull request code, so it is read as data: a regular file only,
  # never a link that would read a file of the runner into the comment.
  def self.artifact_file(dir, name)
    path = File.join(dir, name)
    File.file?(path) && !File.symlink?(path) ? path : nil
  end

  def self.pr_number(dirs)
    path = dirs.filter_map { |d| artifact_file(d, "pr-number") }.first
    raise Error, "no pr-number in #{dirs.join(', ')}" unless path

    text = File.read(path, 32).to_s.sub(/\n\z/, "")
    raise Error, "pr-number is not a pull request number" unless text.match?(/\A[1-9][0-9]*\z/)

    Integer(text, 10)
  end

  KEY = %r{\A[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:/[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)*\z}
  VERDICTS = ["faster", "slower", "likely faster", "likely slower", "noisy", "no change", "new", "removed", "failed"].freeze
  BASE_STATUSES = %w[built missing failed].freeze
  # The longest free-text field that comment renders from an artifact.
  TEXT_LIMIT = 200
  # The largest result.json that comment reads.
  RESULT_LIMIT = 64 << 20

  # Free text of an artifact as plain text: no markup, entity, reference or mention survives. nil for
  # a non-string or an empty result.
  def self.plain(text)
    return nil unless text.is_a?(String)

    text = text.scrub("").gsub(%r{[^A-Za-z0-9 ._\-+()/,@]}, "").strip[0, TEXT_LIMIT]
               .gsub("@", "@\u200b").gsub(/(www)\./i, "\\1\u200b.")
    text.empty? ? nil : text
  end

  def self.number?(v) = v.is_a?(Numeric) && v.to_f.finite?

  def self.number_or_nil(value) = number?(value) ? value : nil

  # A median with both CI bounds or neither, as change() expects.
  def self.stat_of(value)
    return nil unless value.is_a?(Hash) && number?(value["median"])

    bounds = number?(value["lower"]) && number?(value["upper"])
    {median: value["median"], lower: bounds ? value["lower"] : nil, upper: bounds ? value["upper"] : nil}
  end

  def self.comment_row(row)
    return nil unless row.is_a?(Hash) && row["key"].is_a?(String) && row["key"].match?(KEY) && VERDICTS.include?(row["verdict"])

    cpu = row["cpu"].is_a?(Hash) ? row["cpu"] : {}
    ops = row["throughput"].is_a?(Hash) ? row["throughput"] : {}
    {key: row["key"], verdict: row["verdict"], reason: plain(row["reason"]),
     harness_changed: row["harness_changed"] == true, suspect: row["suspect"] == true,
     base: stat_of(row["base"]), head: stat_of(row["head"]), ratio: stat_of(row["ratio"]),
     cpu: {verdict: VERDICTS.include?(cpu["verdict"]) ? cpu["verdict"] : nil,
           base: stat_of(cpu["base"]), head: stat_of(cpu["head"]), ratio: stat_of(cpu["ratio"])},
     throughput: {verdict: VERDICTS.include?(ops["verdict"]) ? ops["verdict"] : nil,
                  base: stat_of(ops["base"]), head: stat_of(ops["head"]), ratio: stat_of(ops["ratio"])}}
  end

  # The result that comment() renders, built from a result.json of an artifact by the checks that the
  # header describes.
  def self.comment_result(raw)
    unless raw.is_a?(Hash) && raw["rows"].is_a?(Array) && raw["base"].is_a?(Hash) && raw["head"].is_a?(Hash)
      raise Error, "result.json is not a result written by bin/check-perf"
    end

    at = ->(*path) { path.reduce(raw) { |v, k| v.is_a?(Hash) ? v[k] : nil } }
    sha = ->(v) { v.is_a?(String) && v.match?(/\A\h{7,40}\z/) ? v : "?" }
    integer = ->(v) { v.is_a?(Integer) ? v : nil }
    keys = ->(v) { v.is_a?(Array) ? v.select { |k| k.is_a?(String) && k.match?(KEY) } : [] }
    status = at.call("base", "status")
    cluster = at.call("metadata", "cluster")
    {base: {sha: sha.call(at.call("base", "sha")), status: BASE_STATUSES.include?(status) ? status : "?",
            reason: plain(at.call("base", "reason"))},
     head: {sha: sha.call(at.call("head", "sha"))},
     # A round count past 100 is left out, which bounds the cost of likely_rate.
     suites: keys.call(raw["suites"]), rounds: integer.call(raw["rounds"])&.then { |n| n.between?(1, 100) ? n : nil },
     metadata: {cpu: {model: plain(at.call("metadata", "cpu", "model")),
                      logical_cores: integer.call(at.call("metadata", "cpu", "logical_cores")),
                      physical_cores: integer.call(at.call("metadata", "cpu", "physical_cores")),
                      governor: plain(at.call("metadata", "cpu", "governor")), smt: at.call("metadata", "cpu", "smt") == true},
                memory: {total_bytes: number_or_nil(at.call("metadata", "memory", "total_bytes"))},
                runner: %w[ImageOS ImageVersion].to_h { |k| [k, plain(at.call("metadata", "runner", k))] },
                compiler: {version: plain(at.call("metadata", "compiler", "version"))},
                build_type: plain(at.call("metadata", "build_type")),
                cluster: cluster.is_a?(Hash) ? {nodes: integer.call(cluster["nodes"]), version: plain(cluster["version"])} : nil},
     rows: raw["rows"].filter_map { |r| comment_row(r) }, skipped: keys.call(raw["skipped"])}
  end

  def self.read_result(path)
    raise Error, "#{path} is larger than #{RESULT_LIMIT} bytes" if File.size(path) > RESULT_LIMIT

    JSON.parse(File.read(path, encoding: "UTF-8"))
  rescue JSON::ParserError => e
    raise Error, "#{path} is not JSON: #{e.message}"
  end

  # The marker line, then the rendered comment of each directory in order, within BODY_LIMIT.
  def self.comment_body(texts)
    body = "#{MARKER}\n#{texts.map(&:rstrip).join("\n\n")}\n"
    return body if body.length <= BODY_LIMIT

    "#{body[0, BODY_LIMIT - TRUNCATED.length]}#{TRUNCATED}"
  end

  # The comment of the marker that the token's bot wrote, so a comment that a user plants with the
  # marker is never edited.
  def self.find_comment(http, repo, number)
    page = 1
    loop do
      comments = http.call(:get, "/repos/#{repo}/issues/#{number}/comments?per_page=100&page=#{page}")
      found = comments.find { |c| c.dig("user", "login") == BOT_LOGIN && c["body"].to_s.start_with?("#{MARKER}\n") }
      return found if found
      return nil if comments.size < 100

      page += 1
    end
  end

  def self.post_comment(dirs, env: ENV, http: nil)
    texts = dirs.filter_map { |d| (f = artifact_file(d, "result.json")) && comment(comment_result(read_result(f))) }
    if texts.empty?
      puts "no result.json in #{dirs.join(', ')}; nothing to post"
      return 0
    end
    number = pr_number(dirs)
    repo = env["GITHUB_REPOSITORY"].to_s
    raise Error, "GITHUB_REPOSITORY is not OWNER/NAME" unless repo.match?(%r{\A[\w.-]+/[\w.-]+\z})

    expected = env["HEAD_SHA"].to_s
    raise Error, "HEAD_SHA is not set" if expected.empty?

    http ||= GitHubHttp.new(env.fetch("GH_TOKEN") { raise Error, "GH_TOKEN is not set" })
    head = http.call(:get, "/repos/#{repo}/pulls/#{number}").dig("head", "sha")
    if head != expected
      puts "superseded by #{head}"
      return 0
    end

    body = {body: comment_body(texts)}
    existing = find_comment(http, repo, number)
    if existing
      http.call(:patch, "/repos/#{repo}/issues/comments/#{existing.fetch('id')}", body)
      puts "edited comment #{existing['id']} of pull request #{number}"
    else
      http.call(:post, "/repos/#{repo}/issues/#{number}/comments", body)
      puts "created a comment on pull request #{number}"
    end
    0
  end

  # Written to perf/index.html in refs/site/pages. Static: it fetches data/index.json and the
  # data/SHA.json of each commit it lists, and draws with inline SVG. REPO_URL is replaced when
  # the page is written.
  PAGE = <<~'HTML'
    <!doctype html>
    <html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
    <title>Performance history</title>
    <style>
      :root { --fg: #1f2328; --bg: #fff; --muted: #656d76; --grid: #d0d7de; --latency: #0969da; --cpu: #bc4c00; --rss: #1a7f37; --throughput: #8250df; }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root { --fg: #e6edf3; --bg: #0d1117; --muted: #8b949e; --grid: #30363d; --latency: #58a6ff; --cpu: #f0883e; --rss: #3fb950; --throughput: #bc8cff; }
      }
      body { font: 14px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif; color: var(--fg); background: var(--bg); max-width: 72rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1rem; }
      nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
      nav a { color: var(--fg); display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem; }
